    hofcbRebuildDetailDesc: 'House of CB is replacing its legacy PHP and Smarty commerce stack — built on CS-Cart, a monolithic e-commerce platform — with a new Next.js 16 system for an international team on a live store where a dress sells roughly every 30 seconds. This rebuild is the system the brand will run going forward for administration, internal operations and the business domain that pricing, stock and orders depend on — not a cosmetic UI refresh or a side experiment alongside the old admin. This role focuses on the admin panel and commerce domain logic — pricing, stock, orders, and internal operations. Work starts from real operational flows mapped with product, then extracts domain parity from the current production system rather than cloning legacy screens: a greenfield build with new architecture and technology that replaces the old platform’s capabilities. Data access goes through Prisma on MySQL. Redis is the shared application cache for hot catalog and expensive domain reads across services and instances; Next.js caching sits on the render and data-fetching layer so those paths do not recompute on every request — two complementary layers, not duplicates. The rebuild treats revenue-critical rules as first-class software: a pricing or inventory mistake costs money at this volume. Explicit domain boundaries also make internal process automation practical — work that was theoretically possible on the old codebase but nearly impossible to ship safely when rules lived scattered across a decade of mixed, multi-author legacy code.',
